WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.681
Pagination is more than a convenience feature.

2
00:00:02.681 --> 00:00:12.737
Page-based and cursor-based retrieval can create partial results,
duplicate records, missing records, inconsistent ordering, or stale views
when data changes during a scan.

3
00:00:12.737 --> 00:00:22.658
A secure client should define how it detects completion, handles empty
pages, limits total records, preserves ordering assumptions, and resumes
safely after interruption.

4
00:00:22.658 --> 00:00:27.820
Poor pagination can cause security decisions to be based on incomplete or
duplicated data.

5
00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:30.904
Rate limits and quotas shape safe behavior under load.

6
00:00:30.904 --> 00:00:40.825
A client should understand how the API signals throttling, whether retry-
after guidance is available, and what limits apply by tenant, credential,
endpoint, or time period.

7
00:00:40.825 --> 00:00:46.792
Ignoring rate limits can create outages, unexpected cost, blocked
credentials, or noisy failure loops.

8
00:00:46.792 --> 00:00:54.099
Treat rate-limit handling as part of the integration contract, especially
when the API supports important business workflows.

9
00:00:54.099 --> 00:00:55.976
Retries need strict discipline.

10
00:00:55.976 --> 00:01:02.411
Connection timeouts, read timeouts, transient errors, and ambiguous
outcomes should be handled deliberately.

11
00:01:02.411 --> 00:01:09.182
Retrying every failure forever can amplify incidents, duplicate actions,
flood dependencies, or hide real problems.

12
00:01:09.182 --> 00:01:20.645
Bounded retries, backoff, jitter concepts, circuit-breaking patterns, and
clear failure reporting help the application recover without turning a
dependency problem into a wider operational issue.

13
00:01:20.645 --> 00:01:23.662
Idempotency is critical when API calls change state.

14
00:01:23.662 --> 00:01:33.785
If a request creates a charge, opens a ticket, approves access, sends a
message, provisions a resource, or records a decision, the client must
avoid unintended duplicate effects.

15
00:01:33.785 --> 00:01:42.432
Idempotency keys, operation identifiers, state checks, and clear
reconciliation behavior help repeated requests represent one intended
operation.

16
00:01:42.432 --> 00:01:51.080
Timeout ambiguity is the hard case: the client may not know whether the
remote side completed the action, so the design must account for that
uncertainty.
